class Solution {
public:
    int differenceOfSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        int ele_sum = 0;
        int dig_sum = 0;
        for(int i=0; i<nums.size(); i++){
            ele_sum = ele_sum + nums[i];
            while(nums[i] != 0){
                int last = nums[i] % 10;
                dig_sum = dig_sum + last;
                nums[i] = nums[i] / 10;
            }
        }
        return abs(ele_sum - dig_sum);
    }
};

review.raw:
VERDICT
Correct: your code correctly computes both the element sum and the digit sum in a single pass using standard modulo and division, but it unnecessarily destroys the values in the input array.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You calculated both sums in a single pass over the array instead of doing two separate loops.

You properly extracted each digit using standard % 10 and / 10 operations.

You used std::abs at the end to guarantee a non-negative difference as required by the problem.

Is it correct?

Yes, it passes all test cases on LeetCode. There are no failing test cases.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

You modified the input vector directly (nums[i] /= 10) inside the while loop, which reduces every element in nums to 0 by the time the function finishes. In interview settings, mutating the input array when not requested is considered poor practice because the caller loses the original data.

The fix is to copy nums[i] into a local temporary variable before running the while loop:

C++
int temp = nums[i];
while (temp != 0) {
    dig_sum += temp % 10;
    temp /= 10;
}
In mathematics, the element sum of positive integers is always greater than or equal to the digit sum (a multi-digit positive number like 25 is strictly larger than 2 + 5 = 7). Therefore, abs() is technically redundant; ele_sum - dig_sum is guaranteed to be non-negative for positive integers.

Edge cases handled properly: single-digit numbers (where element sum and digit sum are identical, difference is 0), arrays with all single-digit numbers (returns 0), and maximum constraint values (numbers up to 2000).

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(n * d) where n is the number of elements and d is the average number of digits per element (at most 4 for nums[i] <= 2000), because each number is divided down digit by digit.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because only three primitive integer variables are stored in memory.

Why my approach works:

Take nums = [1, 15, 6]. For 1: ele_sum becomes 1, dig_sum gets 1. For 15: ele_sum adds 15 (total 16), while loop extracts 5 then 1, so dig_sum adds 6 (total 7). For 6: ele_sum adds 6 (total 22), dig_sum adds 6 (total 13). Final difference is 22 - 13 = 9.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Convert Each Number to String (Brute Force)

Where it comes from:

Common beginner approach found in LeetCode discussion forums and introductory programming lessons.

Idea:

Sum the elements directly. For the digit sum, convert each number into a text string using std::to_string, loop through every character, convert it back into a number with ch - '0', and add it to the digit sum.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <cstdlib>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int differenceOfSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        int ele_sum = 0;
        int dig_sum = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            ele_sum += num;
            string s = to_string(num);
            for (char ch : s) {
                dig_sum += (ch - '0');
            }
        }
        return ele_sum - dig_sum;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n * d) where n is the array length and d is the number of digits, to convert to string and iterate through each character.

Space Complexity:

O(d) auxiliary space per number to store the temporary string buffer.

What makes the next approach better:

Converting integers to strings allocates extra heap/buffer memory. We can extract digits directly using elementary arithmetic (% 10 and / 10) without any allocations.

Approach 2: Two Passes with Separate Accumulation (Clean & Non-Mutating)

Where it comes from:

Standard modular programming style and LeetCode community implementations.

Idea:

Compute the element sum first (or use std::accumulate). Then in a second pass (or helper function), compute the digit sum using arithmetic digit extraction without altering the original array.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>
#include <numeric>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    int getDigitSum(int num) {
        int sum = 0;
        while (num > 0) {
            sum += num % 10;
            num /= 10;
        }
        return sum;
    }
public:
    int differenceOfSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        int ele_sum = accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0);
        int dig_sum = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            dig_sum += getDigitSum(num);
        }
        return ele_sum - dig_sum;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n * d) because accumulate takes O(n) and digit summing takes O(n * d).

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better:

We do not need two separate passes or two separate sum variables. We can maintain a single difference variable in one pass.

Approach 3: Single-Pass Combined Difference (Your Approach Fixed — Optimal)

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial and top-voted solutions.

Idea:

For every number, add the number itself to our running difference and subtract each of its digits. At the end, the difference between the two sums is already computed.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int differenceOfSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        int diff = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            diff += num;
            while (num > 0) {
                diff -= num % 10;
                num /= 10;
            }
        }
        return diff;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n * d) where d is at most 4 (since nums[i] <= 2000), making it effectively O(n) linear time.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space because only one scalar tracker variable (diff) is used.

What makes the next approach better:

Numbers from 1 to 9 have an element value that is identical to their digit sum (e.g., 7 - 7 = 0). We can completely skip the digit extraction loop for all single-digit numbers.

Approach 4: Single-Digit Bypass Optimization (Micro-Optimized)

Where it comes from:

LeetCode discussion optimizations for avoiding division cycles on numbers < 10.

Idea:

If a number is less than 10, num - digit_sum is 0, so it contributes nothing to the final difference. We only run the digit-stripping loop on numbers >= 10.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int differenceOfSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        int diff = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            if (num >= 10) {
                diff += num;
                int temp = num;
                while (temp > 0) {
                    diff -= temp % 10;
                    temp /= 10;
                }
            }
        }
        return diff;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n * d) worst case, but completely skips loop operations for any number in the range 1 to 9.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used Approach 3 (Single-Pass Combined Sums), but you mutated nums[i] directly by reference rather than copying it to a local variable.

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

Interviewers expect Approach 3 or Approach 2. They want to see that you do not convert integers to strings and that you preserve the caller's input array.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: Why is abs() unnecessary in this problem?

Answer: For any positive integer, the number itself is always greater than or equal to the sum of its digits (e.g., a two-digit number 10a + b >= a + b because 9a >= 0). Since all elements in nums are positive, the element sum is always >= digit sum, so ele_sum - dig_sum is never negative.

Question 2: Can the sum overflow a 32-bit signed integer?

Answer: Constraints give nums.length <= 2000 and nums[i] <= 2000. The maximum possible element sum is 2000 * 2000 = 4,000,000, which is well below 32-bit INT_MAX (2,147,483,647). Overflow is impossible here.

Question 3: How would you handle negative numbers if the problem allowed them?

Answer: In C++, modulo on negative numbers yields negative remainders (e.g., -15 % 10 is -5). You would need to take std::abs() of the number before extracting its digits to sum the digit magnitudes properly.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence:

Any number 1 to 9 has a digit sum equal to itself, while numbers >= 10 always exceed their digit sum, which we compute using modulo and division arithmetic.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Do not mutate the input array (nums[i] /= 10) unless the problem specifically asks for an in-place modification.

Use a single running difference variable (diff += num - digit) instead of keeping two separate sum accumulators.

Prefer range-based for loops (for (int num : nums)) over indexed loops for cleaner, modern C++ code.

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

Digit Extraction via Modulo and Division Arithmetic (% 10 and / 10).

THE BEST SOLUTION

C++
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int differenceOfSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        int diff = 0;

        for (int num : nums) {
            diff += num;
            while (num > 0) {
                diff -= num % 10;
                num /= 10;
            }
        }

        return diff;
    }
};
